Use ASP.NET Core's `WebApplicationFactory<Program>` — it hosts your server in-memory with a test `HttpClient`, no real port needed.

## Setup

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing
```

Expose your `Program` class (in your server project):
```csharp
public partial class Program { }
```

## Integration Test

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;

public class HttpServerTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly HttpClient _client;

    public HttpServerTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _client = factory.CreateClient();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task McpEndpoint_AcceptsInitialize()
    {
        var request = new
        {
            jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 1, method = "initialize",
            @params = new
            {
                protocolVersion = "2024-11-05",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test", version = "1.0" }
            }
        };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", request);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task McpEndpoint_InvokesTool()
    {
        // Initialize session first
        var init = new { jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 1, method = "initialize",
            @params = new { protocolVersion = "2024-11-05",
                capabilities = new { },
                clientInfo = new { name = "test", version = "1.0" } } };
        await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", init);

        // Call a tool
        var toolCall = new { jsonrpc = "2.0", id = 2, method = "tools/call",
            @params = new { name = "echo",
                arguments = new { message = "hello" } } };

        var response = await _client.PostAsJsonAsync("/mcp", toolCall);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var body = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
        Assert.Contains("hello", body);
    }
}
```

## How it works

- `WebApplicationFactory` boots your full ASP.NET Core pipeline in-process (middleware, DI, routing — everything)
- `CreateClient()` gives you an `HttpClient` that talks over in-memory transport — no TCP, no port conflicts
- You send raw JSON-RPC requests exactly as a real MCP client would, exercising the full protocol

You can also override DI registrations using `.WithWebHostBuilder(b => b.ConfigureServices(...))` to swap in fakes for external dependencies.
